<worksheet>
    <title>Checkpoint 1</title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>Slope fields analysis</li>
            <li>Applications of differential equations</li>
            <li>Separable equations </li>
            <li>Integrating factor </li>
            <li>Exact Equations </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           Consider the population model
                       <md>
                           \frac{dP}{dt}=-0.2\left(1-\frac{P}{250}\right)\left(\frac{P}{40}-1\right)P,
                       </md>
                       where <m>P(t)</m> is the population at time <m>t</m> 
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               For which values of <m>P</m> is the population in equilibrium? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task >
        <statement >
            <p>
                For which values of <m>P</m> is the population increasing and decreasing?
                                Explain your answer in one sentence.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement >
            <p>
               For which values of <m>P</m> is the population graph concave upwards and
                               downwards?
                               Explain your answer in one sentence. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Sketch the rate vs solution curve, showing the equilibrium solutions and $y-$values where inflection points can occur. Roughly sketch a possible graph of a solution <m>P(t)</m>  where <m>P(0)</m> is in each region of you specified in the previous parts.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            
            
            Suppose that you have a water tank that holds 100 gallons of water.
                        A briny solution <m>A</m>, which contains 20 grams of salt per gallon, enters
                        the tank at a rate of 3 gallons per minute.
                        Another briny solution <m>B</m>, which contains 14 grams of salt per gallon,
                        simultaneously enters the tank at the rate of 7 gallons per minute.
                        At the same time, the solution is well mixed, and water is pumped out of the
                        tank at the rate of 10 gallons per minute.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Since 10 gallons enters the tank every minute and 10 gallons leaves every minute, what can you conclude about the volume of water in the tank?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               How many grams of salt enters the tank every minute from both solutions? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>S(t)</m> denotes the number of grams of salt in the tank in minute <m>t</m>. How many grams are there in each gallon in minute <m>t</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Since water leave the tank at 10 gallons per minute, how many grams of salt leave the tank each minute?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write a differential equation that expresses the total rate of change in
                                <m>S</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Identify any equilibrium solutions and determine whether they are stable or
                                unstable.
                                [A Stable Equilibrium solution means that as <m>t</m> becomes large, <m>S(t)</m>
                                approaches that solution].
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                
                Suppose that there is initially no salt in the tank (i.e. <m>S(0)=0</m>). Find the amount of salt <m>S(t)</m> in minute <m>t</m>(i.e. solve the differential equation from part (e))
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What happens to <m>S(t)</m> after a very long time?
                               <m> \left(
                               \mbox{i.e. Find }\displaystyle{\lim_{t\to\infty}S(t)}\right)</m> 
            </p>
            <hint>
        <p>
            Hint: See page 32 in your textbook.
        </p>
    </hint>
        </statement>
        
    </task>
    
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <introduction>
       <p>
        
       
                            Solve the initial value problem.
                        
            
                     
                            <md>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dt}=2ty^2+3t^2y^2+2t+3t^2}</md>, and
                            <m>y(0)=-1</m>.</p>
                        
    </introduction>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            
                            Solve the initial value problem.
                        
                            <md>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dt}=2ty+4te^{t^2}}</md>, and <m>y(0)=5</m>.
                        
        </p>
    </introduction>
</exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           
                            Solve the initial value problem.
                        <md>\displaystyle{(y\cdot e^{xy}+\cos x)+\left(x\cdot e^{xy}+2y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}=0}</md>,
                                                and <m>y(\pi/2)=0</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>